  "voiceover": "One book — or so we call it. Open it, and you find a whole library: dozens of books, written by around forty authors, across roughly fifteen centuries. Kings and shepherds. A doctor, a tax collector, fishermen, a former enemy of the faith. They wrote in three languages — Hebrew, Aramaic, and Greek. Most of them never met. There was no committee, no shared outline, no way to coordinate across so many centuries. And yet it tells one story. It opens in a garden, with paradise lost, and closes in a garden restored, with the tree of life. A promise made on the very first pages — a deliverer who would crush evil — is traced through law and prophet and psalm, and lands on a single person. Jesus said the Law, the Prophets, and the Psalms were written about him. Forty voices. Fifteen centuries. One unfolding story — reading less like an anthology, and more like the fingerprint of a single Author.",
